"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have rigorously proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines that covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence the requested construction is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **List the required points.**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set  \n   \\[\n   S_3=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify all possible sunny lines that can contain at least two points of \\(S_3\\).**  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Computing the slopes of every unordered pair of points in \\(S_3\\) shows that the only *sunny* slopes occurring are  \n   \\[\n   1,\\qquad -\\tfrac12,\\qquad -2,\n   \\]\n   realised by the three pairs  \n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;(1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;(1,3)-(2,1).\n   \\]\n   No three points of \\(S_3\\) are collinear with any of these slopes, so a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n3. **Bound the number of points that can be covered by the two sunny lines.**  \n   Because each sunny line holds at most two points, the two sunny lines together cover at most four distinct points of \\(S_3\\).  \n   Consequently the remaining two points must lie on the unique non\u2011sunny line.\n\n4. **Exhaust the possible choices of the two sunny lines.**  \n   The only candidates for sunny lines are \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \n   Selecting any two of them leaves exactly the two points that form the third pair:\n   * Choose \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) \u2192 leftover points are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which lie on \\(L_3\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n   * Choose \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_3\\) \u2192 leftover points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which lie on \\(L_2\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n   * Choose \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\) \u2192 leftover points are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), which lie on \\(L_1\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n\n   In every case the line determined by the two uncovered points has a **sunny** slope, contradicting the requirement that the third line be non\u2011sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   No selection of two sunny lines can leave a pair of points whose connecting line is non\u2011sunny. Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{A=(1,1),\\;B=(1,2),\\;C=(1,3),\\;D=(2,1),\\;E=(2,2),\\;F=(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Slopes of all unordered pairs of points in \\(S_3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope} \\\\ \\hline\nA\\!-\\!B & \\infty \\\\\nA\\!-\\!C & \\infty \\\\\nA\\!-\\!D & 0 \\\\\nA\\!-\\!E & 1 \\\\\nA\\!-\\!F & 0 \\\\\nB\\!-\\!C & \\infty \\\\\nB\\!-\\!D & -1 \\\\\nB\\!-\\!E & 0 \\\\\nB\\!-\\!F & -\\tfrac12 \\\\\nC\\!-\\!D & -2 \\\\\nC\\!-\\!E & -1 \\\\\nC\\!-\\!F & -1 \\\\\nD\\!-\\!E & \\infty \\\\\nD\\!-\\!F & 0 \\\\\nE\\!-\\!F & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes that are **sunny** (i.e. different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) are therefore  \n\n\\[\n1,\\qquad -\\tfrac12,\\qquad -2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the only pairs of points that can lie on a sunny line are  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;A\\!-\\!E\\;(1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad\nL_2:\\;B\\!-\\!F\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad\nL_3:\\;C\\!-\\!D\\;(1,3)-(2,1).\n\\]\n\nNo three points of \\(S_3\\) share any of these three slopes, so a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Distribution of the six points among three lines\n\nWe must use exactly three distinct lines, two of them sunny, to cover all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nBecause each sunny line holds at most two points, the two sunny lines together cover at most four distinct points. Hence at least two points of \\(S_3\\) are not on any sunny line; they must lie on the unique non\u2011sunny line.\n\nThe non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore its slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Exhaustion of possible sunny\u2011line choices\n\nThe only possible sunny lines that contain two points of \\(S_3\\) are \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\) listed above.  \nWe consider all \\(\\binom{3}{2}=3\\) ways to pick two of them as the sunny lines.\n\n* **Case 1:** Sunny lines \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \n  Points covered: \\(A,E\\) (by \\(L_1\\)) and \\(B,F\\) (by \\(L_2\\)).  \n  Remaining points: \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n  The line through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is precisely \\(L_3\\), whose slope is \\(-2\\) (sunny).  \n  Hence the third line would be sunny, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n* **Case 2:** Sunny lines \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_3\\).  \n  Points covered: \\(A,E\\) and \\(C,D\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \n  The line through \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) is \\(L_2\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), sunny).  \n  Again the third line would be sunny \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **Case 3:** Sunny lines \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\).  \n  Points covered: \\(B,F\\) and \\(C,D\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(A\\) and \\(E\\).  \n  The line through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) is \\(L_1\\) (slope \\(1\\), sunny).  \n  Contradiction as before.\n\nIn every possible selection, the two points left uncovered by the sunny lines determine a line whose slope is sunny. Therefore the third line cannot be non\u2011sunny, and a configuration with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n\nNo arrangement of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition for the set \\(S_3\\) while having exactly two of the lines sunny.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
