\)* (i.e. \\(n=3t+2\\)): the uncovered points are \\((t+1,1)\\) and\n   \\((t+1,2)\\).  They are covered by the two lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}: x=t+1 ,\\qquad L_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(2t+2=\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil\\).\n\n   In every case we have constructed exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   distinct non\u2011sunny lines that cover **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n4. **Adding the remaining lines and controlling the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   The total number of lines required is \\(n\\).  After the construction of step\u202f3 we\n   still need  \n\n   \\[\n   r_{\\text{add}} = n-m_{\\min}= n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   further distinct lines.  We exhibit two explicit families of lines that are\n   guaranteed to be distinct from all lines already used and from each other.\n\n   *Sunny family.*  \n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,r_{\\text{add}}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = (j+1)x + j .\\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(j+1\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  Different values of \\(j\\) give different slopes, so the lines\n   are distinct.  None of the lines (8) coincides with any vertical, horizontal or\n   diagonal line used in step\u202f3, because those have slopes \\(0\\), undefined or \\(-1\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny family (if needed).*  \n   If we wish to add a non\u2011sunny line instead of a sunny one, we may use  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x = n+1+j,\\qquad\n   H_j:\\; y = n+1+j,\\qquad\n   D_j:\\; x+y = n+2+j .\\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of these is respectively vertical, horizontal or diagonal, hence non\u2011sunny,\n   and all constants are larger than any constant appearing in step\u202f3, guaranteeing\n   distinctness.\n\n   To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le r_{\\text{add}}\\))\n   we proceed as follows:\n\n   * Choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n   * Choose the remaining \\(r_{\\text{add}}-k\\) lines from the non\u2011sunny family (9)\n     (any selection works because they are all distinct and do not affect the\n     covering property).\n\n   The final set consists of the \\(m_{\\min}\\) non\u2011sunny lines from step\u202f3 together\n   with the \\(r_{\\text{add}}\\) extra lines just described; it contains exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines and \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   From step\u202f2 we have the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-m_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Step\u202f4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   a concrete configuration exists.  Hence these and only these values of \\(k\\) are\n   admissible.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le\\left\\lfloor\\dfrac{n}{3}\\right\\rfloor\\text{ are possible, and no other }k\\text{ works.}}\n\\]"

