) that satisfy  \n\n\\[\nmx+b\\in\\mathbb Z\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;p x\\equiv -qb\\pmod q .\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible \\(x\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(q\\);\nconsequently there are at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such \\(x\\).\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le s\\).\n\nIf \\(q=1\\) the slope \\(m\\) is an integer different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\nFor an integer slope the condition \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n1\\le mx+b\\le n+1-x .\n\\]\n\nBoth inequalities are linear in \\(x\\); solving them shows that the set of admissible\n\\(x\\) is an interval of length at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Hence the number of integer\n\\(x\\) in the interval is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=s\\).\n\nIn every case the line contains at most \\(s\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of too many sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le s=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together contain at most \\(k s\\) points of \\(T_n\\) by Lemma 1.\nAll remaining \\(|T_n|-k s\\) points must be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines,\nwhich are non‑sunny.  A non‑sunny line belongs to one of the families (1); each such\nline contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the longest column \\(x=1\\) has exactly\n\\(n\\) points).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-k s\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 -k s\\le n^2-nk\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non‑sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nThe points that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a = k+1-t\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(n-k+t,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le t\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-t\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\); it contains\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x-d .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(L_d\\) is sunny.\nInside the triangle \\(U\\) the equation \\(y=x-d\\) picks out exactly the points with\n\\(a-b=d\\); those are precisely the points of one “diagonal’’ of \\(U\\).\nWhen \\(d\\) runs from \\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\) the lines \\(L_d\\) are distinct and together\ncover all points of \\(U\\).\n\n*Counting.*  \nWe have used \\((n-k)\\) lines of type (2) and \\(k\\) lines of type (3), altogether\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  Hence the required\nconfiguration exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLemma 2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lf